// isCleanEOF reports whether err denotes only end-of-input: io.EOF itself,
// or wrappers (single or joined) whose every underlying cause is io.EOF.
// A combined error that pairs io.EOF with any other independent cause is a
// read fault, not a clean end.
func isCleanEOF(err error) bool {
    if err == nil {
        return false
    }
    if err == io.EOF {
        return true
    }
    switch u := err.(type) {
    case interface{ Unwrap() []error }:
        for _, e := range u.Unwrap() {
            if !isCleanEOF(e) {
                return false
            }
        }
        return true
    case interface{ Unwrap() error }:
        return isCleanEOF(u.Unwrap())
    }
    return false
}
```

Wait — should use `errors.Is(err, io.EOF)` at leaf? A custom error type could match io.EOF via Is. Hmm, keep it simple: leaf check `err == io.EOF || errors.Is(err, io.EOF)`? errors.Is would recurse into Unwrap anyway. For a leaf with no Unwrap, errors.Is(err, io.EOF) checks err == io.EOF or err.Is(io.EOF). Using errors.Is at the leaf is fine and handles custom Is. But careful: if I call errors.Is at the top it recurses the whole tree and returns true if ANY node matches — that's the bug. So walk manually, and at leaves use errors.Is (which for a leaf without Unwrap is just equality/Is method). Actually simpler: at each node, if errors.Is(node, io.EOF) — no wait, that recurses.

